      "text_raw": "This smooth transition\nbetween weak and strong\ninversion is best\ndescribed by the EKV\nmodel [Enz], explained in\nthis slide. It uses a function\nwhich contains the square of\na natural log function of an\nexponential. The variable is\nv, which is V −V , nor-\nGS T\nmalized to a quantity V\nGSTt\nor simply 2nkT/q. This\nincludes factor n, which\ndepends on some biasing\nvoltages, which is usually\nbetween 70 and 80 mV.",
      "text": "This smooth transition between weak and strong inversion is best described by the EKV model [Enz], explained in this slide. It uses a function which contains the square of a natural log function of an exponential. The variable is v, which is V −V , nor- GS T malized to a quantity V GSTt or simply 2nkT/q. This includes factor n, which depends on some biasing voltages, which is usually between 70 and 80 mV."
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 21,
      "printed_page": 21,
      "bbox_pt": [
